{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576642905,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyNjYxMzQ5MQ=="},"target":"10f8c64d4571e8baebdceda2136bde60c20025da92b51eeb00c88d08aba6d711","message":"Minimal example. You can see that on 2nd run of `save` it would add that file.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003elonger example - but apparently superds is not needed to exhibit bug\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n```\n/tmp \u003e datalad create superds \u0026\u0026 cd superds \u0026\u0026 datalad create -d . testds \u0026\u0026 cd testds \u0026\u0026 mkdir -p subds1 \u0026\u0026 touch subds1/file \u0026\u0026 datalad create -f -d subds1\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/superds \ncreate(ok): /tmp/superds (dataset)                                                                                               \n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/superds/testds \nadd(ok): testds (file)                                                                                                           \nadd(ok): .gitmodules (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\ncreate(ok): testds (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  create (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/superds/testds/subds1 \ncreate(ok): . (dataset)                                                                                                          \n\n/tmp/superds/testds \u003e datalad save -r -d^ subds1\nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nadd(ok): .gitmodules (file)\nsave(ok): testds (dataset)\nadd(ok): testds (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 3)\n  save (ok: 2)\n\n/tmp/superds/testds \u003e datalad save -r -d^ subds1\nadd(ok): file (file)\nsave(ok): testds/subds1 (dataset)\nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nsave(ok): testds (dataset)\nadd(ok): testds (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 3)\n  save (ok: 3)\n\n/tmp/superds/testds \u003e datalad save -r -d^ subds1\naction summary:\n  save (notneeded: 3)\n\n/tmp/superds/testds \u003e datalad --version\ndatalad 0.12.0rc6.dev198\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n```shell\nexit:1 /tmp \u003e datalad create testds \u0026\u0026 cd testds \u0026\u0026 mkdir -p subds1 \u0026\u0026 touch subds1/file \u0026\u0026 datalad create -f -d subds1 \n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/testds \ncreate(ok): /tmp/testds (dataset)                                                                                                \n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/testds/subds1 \ncreate(ok): . (dataset)                                                                                                          \n\n/tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -r -d^ subds1                                                                               \nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nadd(ok): .gitmodules (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  save (ok: 1)\n\n/tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -r -d^ subds1\nadd(ok): file (file)\nsave(ok): subds1 (dataset)\nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  save (ok: 2)\n```\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\nand it is the `-d` specified which affects it (`-d.` is as \"good\"):\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n```shell\n/tmp \u003e datalad create testds \u0026\u0026 cd testds \u0026\u0026 mkdir -p subds1 \u0026\u0026 touch subds1/file \u0026\u0026 datalad create -f -d subds1\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/testds \ncreate(ok): /tmp/testds (dataset)                                                                                                \n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/testds/subds1 \ncreate(ok): . (dataset)                                                                                                          \n\n/tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -d. -r subds1                                                                               \nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nadd(ok): .gitmodules (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  save (ok: 1)\n\n/tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -d. -r subds1\nadd(ok): file (file)\nsave(ok): subds1 (dataset)\nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  save (ok: 2)\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576643116,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Njg2MTU4Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-566861583"},"message":"I guess it is because upon initial invocation of `save`, `subds` is not yet a known to it subdataset, so `-r` is not in effect.  But I would have expected that it would save (probably first within since it is to be committed, but could be first saved to be added, and then saved within, and then saved new state on top).\nThe same happens even without pointing to it with `save -r .` which should in theory save everything:\n```\n/tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -r                                                                                          \nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nadd(ok): .gitmodules (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  save (ok: 1)\n\n/tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -r\nadd(ok): file (file)\nsave(ok): subds1 (dataset)\nadd(ok): subds1 (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 2)\n  save (ok: 2)\n```","files":null},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576643147,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50Mjg5MjE2MjE0NQ=="},"title":"save -r doesn't save inside yet to be added (in the same call) subdataset, requires second call","was":"save -r doesn't save inside yet to be added (in the same call) subdataset, requires second call"},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576643165,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI4OTIxNjI1MDI="},"added":["external-tools"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576643176,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50Mjg5MjE2MjcyMA=="},"added":[],"removed":["external-tools"]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576643176,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI4OTIxNjI3MjE="},"added":["severity-normal"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576643280,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Njg2MjA2NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-566862064"},"message":"0.11.x \"analogue\" `add` works out this case correctly\n```\n$\u003e datalad add -d^ -r subds1                                                                               \nadd(ok): subds1/file (file)\nadd(ok): subds1 (dataset)\nadd(ok): subds1 (dataset) [added new subdataset]\nsave(ok): /tmp/testds/subds1 (dataset)\nsave(ok): /tmp/testds (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (notneeded: 2, ok: 3)\n  save (ok: 2)\n\n$\u003e datalad add -d^ -r subds1\n$\u003e\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576681028,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Njg2ODY0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-566868643"},"message":"IIRC that is an intentional change.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576681028,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyNjkxMTYzOQ=="},"target":"da1ffbaa8cd81fc95cbe7975ab2a62c9e3cda5e2e5bf9b7ecaaaa23404de0a81","message":"IIRC that is an intentional change.\n\nWhen I say `datalad publish -r` I also do not get/want(?) unregistered subdatasets published. In general, `--recursive` is about recursion into subdatasets, not into directories that may contain other nested standalone datasets. The old code did that, at the expense of some substantial code complexity, because it would recursively dig itself done a directory hierarchy (while trying to maintain the recursion count of course).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576715630,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NzI3NTE5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-567275195"},"message":"mnah, comparing `datalad save` to `publish` here is like comparing `git add` to `push`. Sure thing `push` shouldn't (and will not) `push` unregistered files neither on the first, nor on the second (re)invocation. But the sole role of `save` (as a replacement of `add` and the ultimate ideologist \"from clean state to clean state\" tracking) is to save all the changes to get to a clean state in a single invocation and not a chain of a sufficiently long identical `save`s calls until it finally arrives to a clean state. And `-r` here is the (only) way to say for everything, including submodules. \nSo, if there was any intention, it feels to be more implementation- not a usecase- (or \"clean semantic\") driven.  Unless there was actually some use case such behavior helped to address?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576855439,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Nzk2MTk4Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-567961982"},"message":"I don't think it is useful to employ git analogies to figure out what datalad should do. We are talking about functionality that git doesn't have AFAIK (`git submodule foreach` doesn't seem to touch non-submodule repositories.\n\nI take what you are saying as the proposal to overload `--recursive` (generally being recursion into subdatasets, because directory recursion within datasets is typically implied and generally unavoidable), with also being recursion into subdirectories, when it could make sense, but needs to be enabled, although typically implied.\n\nSo when is directory-recursion into non-subdatasets OK when uniformity for `--recursion` is not OK?\n\n- [ ] aggregate-metadata\n- [ ] diff\n- [ ] drop\n- [ ] get\n- [ ] install\n- [ ] ls (already advertises recursion as \"subdirectory recursion\")\n- [ ] metadata\n- [ ] publish\n- [ ] remove\n- [ ] save\n- [ ] siblings\n- [ ] status\n- [ ] subdatasets\n- [ ] uninstall\n- [ ] unlock,\n- [ ] update","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576855439,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyODIyNDM5OQ=="},"target":"720e0f21bc4e210314359dd1a38a62532f40ce98dfeced65ac25679b18fa63d6","message":"I don't think it is useful to employ git analogies to figure out what datalad should do. We are talking about functionality that git doesn't have AFAIK (`git submodule foreach` doesn't seem to touch non-submodule repositories.\n\nI take what you are saying as the proposal to overload `--recursive` (generally being recursion into subdatasets, because directory recursion within datasets is typically implied and generally unavoidable), with also being recursion into subdirectories, when it could make sense, but needs to be enabled, although typically implied.\n\nSo when is directory-recursion into non-subdatasets OK when uniformity for `--recursion` is not OK?\n\n- [ ] aggregate-metadata\n- [ ] diff\n- [ ] drop\n- [ ] get\n- [ ] install\n- [x] ls (already advertises recursion as \"subdirectory recursion\")\n- [ ] metadata\n- [ ] publish\n- [ ] remove\n- [ ] save\n- [ ] siblings\n- [ ] status\n- [ ] subdatasets\n- [ ] uninstall\n- [ ] unlock,\n- [ ] update","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576855468,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyODIyNDY1Nw=="},"target":"720e0f21bc4e210314359dd1a38a62532f40ce98dfeced65ac25679b18fa63d6","message":"I don't think it is useful to employ git analogies to figure out what datalad should do. We are talking about functionality that git doesn't have AFAIK (`git submodule foreach` doesn't seem to touch non-submodule repositories).\n\nI take what you are saying as the proposal to overload `--recursive` (generally being recursion into subdatasets, because directory recursion within datasets is typically implied and generally unavoidable), with also being recursion into subdirectories, when it could make sense, but needs to be enabled, although typically implied.\n\nSo when is directory-recursion into non-subdatasets OK when uniformity for `--recursion` is not OK?\n\n- [ ] aggregate-metadata\n- [ ] diff\n- [ ] drop\n- [ ] get\n- [ ] install\n- [x] ls (already advertises recursion as \"subdirectory recursion\")\n- [ ] metadata\n- [ ] publish\n- [ ] remove\n- [ ] save\n- [ ] siblings\n- [ ] status\n- [ ] subdatasets\n- [ ] uninstall\n- [ ] unlock,\n- [ ] update","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576855505,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyODIyNDk3Ng=="},"target":"720e0f21bc4e210314359dd1a38a62532f40ce98dfeced65ac25679b18fa63d6","message":"I don't think it is useful to employ git analogies to figure out what datalad should do. We are talking about functionality that git doesn't have AFAIK (`git submodule foreach` doesn't seem to touch non-submodule repositories).\n\nI take what you are saying as the proposal to overload `--recursive` (generally being recursion into subdatasets, because directory recursion within datasets is typically implied and generally unavoidable), with also being recursion into subdirectories, when it could make sense, but needs to be enabled, although typically implied.\n\nSo when is directory-recursion into non-subdatasets OK when uniformity for `--recursive` is not OK?\n\n- [ ] aggregate-metadata\n- [ ] diff\n- [ ] drop\n- [ ] get\n- [ ] install\n- [x] ls (already advertises recursion as \"subdirectory recursion\")\n- [ ] metadata\n- [ ] publish\n- [ ] remove\n- [ ] save\n- [ ] siblings\n- [ ] status\n- [ ] subdatasets\n- [ ] uninstall\n- [ ] unlock,\n- [ ] update","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576858814,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Nzk4MTU3Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-567981577"},"message":"so far recursion into subdirectories is needed only for `save` (per original point of this issue).  So far I do not think is pertinent to any other command (but if I mark them in your checklist - we wouldn't know if it was you or not ;))\nMay be `ls` but that one is an antique alien, meaning of `-r` in which ATM depends on what it is provided with -- for datasets - would explore subdatasets, for directories and files - does not recurse at all (from top level interface at least), for s3 urls - would recurse into \"directories\" (there is no directories per se on s3).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576859362,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Nzk4NDk2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-567984965"},"message":"What should a `--recursion-limit` limit do in case of `--recursive` meaning recursion into subdirectories?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576860356,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Nzk5MDQwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-567990406"},"message":"nothing.  my view: `--recursion` (and its limit) is about subdatasets and (at least ATM) it should stay that way. I do not think it would be productive to dive into all \"subdatasets vs subdirectories\" semantics/discussion here.\nEven now `save -r` alters the listing of subdatasets, but does not care to apply `save` to the subdataset it just added (2nd invocation does it).  So IMHO a solution to this particular issue could be  for `save` internally trigger `save`'ing freshly added (by it) subdatasets (if ran with `--recursive` and respecting the limit) -- so it would not solely rely on listing of `subdatasets` as when initially invoked.  The simplest solution would generate two commits in the superdataset -- one for adding subdataset (as is), then 2nd one (possibly, if subdataset was dirty) is to save updated state on the dataset after it calls `save -r` within that dataset (adjusting recursion limit how it already does).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1576860750,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2Nzk5MjU2MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3943#issuecomment-567992561"},"message":"BTW, just spotted in\n\n\u003e ```shell\n\u003e /tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -r -d^ subds1                                                                               \n\u003e add(ok): subds1 (file)\n\u003e add(ok): .gitmodules (file)\n\u003e ...\n\u003e ```\n\nis incorrectly lists `subds1` as a `(file)`. It is not, either in human or git (where it would be \"Subproject\" or smth like that IIRC)  or datalad (it is a dataset already, just was not a sub-dataset) terms.  Ideally should be\n\n\u003e ```shell\n\u003e /tmp/testds \u003e datalad save -r -d^ subds1                                                                               \n\u003e add(ok): .gitmodules (file)\n\u003e add(ok): subds1 (dataset)\n\u003e ...\n\u003e ```\n\n(note that 0.11.x was reporting it as a dataset as well). I also reordered (not sure if easy/feasible) to make it even more kosher, i.e. that by the time `add` reported on `subds1`, `.gitmodules` also add'ed.  But that is minor and could be ignored -- it doesn't change the nature of subds1 by itself being a dataset","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578828046,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50Mjk0MTE3MTgxNQ=="},"added":[],"removed":["severity-normal"]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578828046,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5NDExNzE4MTY="},"added":["severity-wishlist"],"removed":[]}]}